    {
      "ordinal": 19,
      "depth": 1,
      "kind": "rule",
      "number": "4.1",
      "title": "Historical sources and datation",
      "body": "● A gorget was a type of neck protection worn over the neck, clavicles, and shoulders. It evolved from earlier forms of neck defenses, which often relied on chainmail or padded fabrics like the aventail. By the late 15th century, as plate armor became more refined, the gorget emerged as a key piece of protection for the throat and neck, made primarily from steel plates. Gorgets were most common in the 16th century but evidence exists of gorgets and plate collars.\n● They are denied for 14th century and Transitional armors\n● They are approved for 15th century armors\n\nWade Allen collection - 1510 - Italy",
      "page_from": 8,
      "page_to": 8,
      "stable_key": "neck-protection:4.1",
      "parent_key": "neck-protection:4",
      "review_status": "extracted",
      "review_note": null,
